<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haeinsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">802</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haeinsa is a major Buddhist temple on Mount Gaya in South Gyeongsang province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, founded in 802. Since 1398 it has kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a>, more than 81,000 woodblocks of the Buddhist canon carved in the thirteenth century. The halls built to store them, the Janggyeong Panjeon, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haeinsa.jpg" alt="Haeinsa" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 한국교육방송공사 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ED%95%9C%EA%B5%AD_%EB%AC%B8%ED%99%94_%EC%97%AC%ED%96%89_%EC%9D%8C%EC%8B%9D_%ED%95%B4%EC%9D%B8%EC%82%AC003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
